ement and it was a  brilliant idea of in a country with a  lot of young people in very few old  people with a high birth rate and a high  death rate to guarantee benefits and  skip a generation by simply taking the  money from young people and giving it to  old people and the old people would get  a benefit they never paid for the only  problem with that program and we have it  is it creates no wealth and it doesn't  benefit from what Einstein called the  most powerful force in the universe  compound interest so now we're closing  in on two workers for every retiree and  it can't work and there's no tax rate at  which it will work that society won't  collapse under its weight and so what  we've got to do the following things in  my opinion number one we've got to  continue phasing up the retirement age  at its current rate not to 67 but 270  number two we've got to index Social  Security in the future not by the wage  rate but by prices private pensions  r-nev indexed by prices those are  changes that are no-brainers in my  and you're not cutting social security  you're just not increasing it as much in  Medicare you've got to have a system  where you got co-payments and  deductibles based on income so for  example I would be covered by Medicare  but I'd have to spend fifty thousand  dollars in one year before the  government started paying and it would  then pay 25 cents on the dollar and the  poorest person in the country might have  a hundred-dollar deductible and a two  dollar co-payment but everybody would  have a deductible and a co-payment and  you would adjust the deductible the  co-payment based on the cost of the  program and if people want to stay in  the old program under that system they  could if they wanted to go into Paul  Ryan's program and have insurance like  Congress they could I don't think  raising the retirement age on the  Medicare is a good idea it's one thing  for me to keep working when I'm old I  don't know anything but work but I find  w